# {{lb|en|countable|slang}} A [[hoax]], [[jest]], or [[prank]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Ferdinando Killigrew|title=The Universal Jester: or, A Pocket Companion for the Wits|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} R. Whitworth,{{nb...|at the Feathers, in the Poultry}}; J. Warcus,{{nb...|at the Indian-Queen, opposite the Mansion-House}}; R. Richards,{{nb...|next Barnard’s-Inn, Holborn}}; W. Mynors,{{nb...|at the Corner of Chancery-Lane, Holborn}}; and W. Heard,{{nb...|at the Philobiblian’s-Library, Piccadilly}}|year=1754|section=title page|oclc=642524111|passage=The universal jester: or, a pocket companion for the wits. Being a choice collection of merry conceits, facetious Drolleries, humorous Waggeries, smart Repartees, pleasant Jokes, Clenchers, Closures, Bon Mots, and '''Humbugs'''; comic Stories, notable Puns, witty Quibbles, and ridiculous Bulls. To which are added, Mr. Puzzlewit's gimcracks ; or, A long String of out-o'th'-way Conundrums, diverting Rebusses, poignant Epigrams, odd and uncommon Epitaphs, &c. &c. All calculated to promote inoffensive Mirth, and divert good Company with Elegance and Taste. Containing more in Number, and greater Variety, than any Book of the Kind yet published. Humbly inscribed to the choice spirits of the age. By Ferdinando Killigrew, Esq.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=A Short Dissertation on the Modern Art of Humbugging|magazine=The Covent-Garden Magazine; or, Amorous Repository:{{nb...|Calculated Solely for the Entertainment of the Polite World, and the Finishing of a Young Gentleman’s Education}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for the authors; and sold by}} G. Allen,{{nb...|No. 59, in Pater-noster Row, and all other booksellers in Great-Britain and Ireland.}}|month=November|year=1772|volume=I|pages=175–176|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=akpGAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA175|oclc=645711122|passage=The profeſſor of the modern '''''Humbugg''''', for ſuch is the polite name of this qualification, muſt either have from nature an unalterable countenance, or from art a power of commanding all its ſucceſſive variations, and preſerving it inviolably in each, as long as the present ſituation of the caſe renders it neceſſary: he muſt have a head full of imagination, and a heart empty of every trace of candor and humanity.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|slang}} A [[fraud]] or [[sham#Noun|sham]]; {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[hypocrisy]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|editor=[J. S. Boone]|title=On Humbug, Pro and Con—and the Art of Puffing|magazine=The Council of Ten|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for the proprietors, and published by}} Thomas Wilkie,{{nb...|Paternoster-Row; [''et al.'']}}|volume=I|issue=III|month=August|year=1822|page=327|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=62wEAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA327|oclc=15101686|passage=Look at the affairs of nations on the widest scale—look at their intercourse with each other—look at the manifestoes, by which war is declared—look at the treaties, by which peace is restored—look at the professions of kings, or popes, or generals, or ministers. Is not cant, '''humbug''', hypocrisy, the staple of them all? What is modern diplomacy, but a system of duplicity and deceit?}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=“C.”|title=Humbug|editor=[[w:George Petrie (artist)|George Petrie]]|magazine=The Irish Penny Journal.{{nb...|Containing Original Contributions by Several of the Most Eminent Irish Writers}}|location=Dublin|publisher={{...|Printed and published by}} Gunn and Cameron,{{nb...|at the office of the General Advertiser, No. 6, Church-Lane, College-Green.}}|date=29 August 1840|year_published=1841|volume=I|issue=9|page=67|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=IefmcuV6F5YC&pg=PA67|column=1|oclc=605070893|passage=What is the civility of the landlord and his waiters but '''humbug'''? What the smirking, smiling, ducking and bowing of the shopkeeper, but '''humbug'''? What his sweet and gentle "yes, sirs," and "no, sirs," and "proud to serve you, sirs," but '''humbug'''? You are not goose enough to believe for a moment that he is serious, that he has either the least regard or respect for you.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=J[oseph] H. Bagg|chapter=Magnetism as More Particularly Applied to Man, or What is Commonly Called Animal Magnetism, Clairvoyance, Catalepsy, Palsy, &c.|title=Bagg on Magnetism, or The Doctrine of Equilibrium:{{nb...|Being an Attempt to Prove that not only the Health of Vegetables and Animals, but All Systems and Principles in Nature, Depend on an Equilibrium or Action between Two Extremes, and that the Impulse or Force by which They are Produced, as well as Destroyed, is Owing to the Magnetic Fluids which Operate both at the Same Time, on the Same Object or Principle. [...] In Two Parts.}}|location=Detroit, Mich.|publisher=Bagg and Harmon,{{nb...|printers}}|year=1845|pages=170–171|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=0uU0AAAAMAAJ&pg=PA171|oclc=565269669|passage=Many times a whole audience will not only be crowded into a small room, but are noisy disbelievers, call it all a '''humbug''', distract the mind of the magnetizer, and added to these, absolutely outwill the magnetizer, in their wish to bring odium upon the science, and carry their points and gain their ends.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Todd Nathan Thompson|chapter=‘Satire upon All of Us’: The Self-made Man as Confidence Man in P. T. Barnum’s America|title=Modest Proposals: American Satire and Political Change from Franklin to Lincoln|series=unpublished Ph.D. in English dissertation|location=Chicago, Ill.|publisher=w:University of Illinois Chicago|year=2008|page=215|oclc=262556595|passage=[[w:P. T. Barnum|[Phineas Taylor] Barnum]] turned profits detecting '''humbug''', staging '''humbugs''', and in authoring books that present him as a '''humbug'''. In each case he operated by aestheticizing '''humbug''': in writing tongue-in-cheek "reform" literature about avoiding '''humbug''', in creating narratives or mythologies to advertise his own '''humbugs''', and in celebrating in prose his own ability to balance contradictory roles.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|slang}} A [[cheat#Noun|cheat]], [[fraudster]], or [[hypocrite]].
#* {{RQ:Disraeli Sybil|volume=I|chapter=XI|page=235|passage=He is a political '''humbug''', the greatest of all '''humbugs'''; a man who swaggers about London clubs and consults solemnly about his influence, and in the country is a nonentity.}}
#* {{RQ:Thackeray Pendennis||passage=All poets are '''humbugs''', all literary men are '''humbugs'''; directly a man begins to sell his feelings for money he’s a '''humbug'''.|42}}
#* {{RQ:Sewell Black Beauty|chapter=A Humbug!|page=148|passage=[I]n a few days my new groom came. He was a tall, good-looking fellow enough; but if ever there was a '''humbug''' in the shape of a groom, Alfred Smirk was the man. He was very civil to me, and never used me ill; in fact, he did a great deal of stroking and patting when his master was there to see it. {{...}} [B]ut as to cleaning my feet, or looking to my shoes, or grooming me thoroughly, he thought no more of that than if I had been a cow.}}
#* {{RQ:Baum Wizard of Oz|chapter=15. The Discovery of Oz, the Terrible|page=184|passage=“Hush, my dear,” he said. “Don’t speak so loud, or you will be overheard—and I should be ruined. I’m supposed to be a Great Wizard.” “And aren’t you?” she asked. “Not a bit of it, my dear; I’m just a common man.” “You’re more than that,” said the Scarecrow, in a grieved tone; “you’re a '''humbug'''.” “Exactly so!” declared the little man.}}
#* {{RQ:Shaw Man and Superman|pages=78–79|pageref=79|passage=He means that he has sold out to the parliamentary '''humbugs''' and the bourgeoisie. Compromise! that is his faith.}}
#* {{RQ:Doyle Lost World|chapter=‘To-morrow We Disappear into the Unknown’|page=104|passage="It is an open admission," he cried. "What more do you want? The fellow is a self-confessed '''humbug'''. We have only to return home and report him as the brazen imposter that he is."}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|slang}} [[nonsense|Nonsense]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Nina Bawden|chapter=3|title=Humbug|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Clarion Books, {{w|Houghton Mifflin Harcourt}}|year=1992|page=17|isbn=978-0-395-62149-3|passage=When they had gone, Ma Potter opened her eyes. She said, "Pay no attention, child. Don't upset yourself. Just '''humbug''', that's all." / "What do you mean?" Cora whispered. {{...}} "You mean, telling lies?" / "Not altogether. Humbuggery is what people talk without thinking. Lies are deliberate. Are you a clever child?"}}
# {{lb|en|countable|Britain}} A type of [[hard]] [[sweet]] ([[candy]]), usually [[peppermint]] [[flavoured]] with a [[striped]] pattern.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Michael Morpurgo|chapter=How to Make Old-fashioned Peppermint Humbugs|title=w:Private Peaceful|location=London|publisher=w:HarperCollins|year=2003|isbn=978-0-00-715007-6|passage='''Humbugs''' are sweet, hard candies with a mild peppermint flavor, which are traditionally made in small batches by hand. {{...}} '''Humbugs''' often feature the old-fashioned peppermint-stripe coloration, dark brown and off-white; are usually oblong or square (about the size of your thumb); {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Jo Cotterill|chapter=White Clover|title=Sweet Hearts|series=Forget Me Not|location=London|publisher=Red Fox|year=2011|page=216|isbn=978-1-84941-217-9|passage=At half past five in the evening, Anpa sat up in bed and said he'd like a packet of '''humbugs'''. Nick and Kate looked at each other and grinned, and Nick immediately picked up his jacket and said he'd go and find some. 'Is he allowed them?' Kate whispered so that Anpa couldn't hear her. / Nick shrugged. 'Can't see how '''humbugs''' will do any harm. But I might get soft mints instead so he doesn't choke on them.'}}
